✅ An Identifier is a user-defined name used to identify variables, functions, arrays, structures, and other program elements in C.
✅ C is Case-Sensitive
    int age = 20;
    int Age = 30;
    int AGE = 40;



    #include <stdio.h>
    int main()
    {
        int age = 21;
        float salary = 35000.50;
        char grade = 'A';
        printf("Age = %d\n", age);
        printf("Salary = %.2f\n", salary);
        printf("Grade = %c\n", grade);
        return 0;
    }

Summary
-------
👉 An identifier is a user-defined name in a C program.
👉 Identifiers are used for variables, functions, arrays, structures, etc.
👉 They must start with a letter or underscore (_).
👉 Digits are allowed after the first character.
👉 Spaces and special characters are not allowed.
👉 Keywords cannot be used as identifiers.
👉 C identifiers are case-sensitive.
👉 Use meaningful and readable names such as studentName or total_marks.

    #include <stdio.h>
